- Story & Context: When hydroponics technician Yaa Santiago discovered a corrupted digital letter from her grandmother back in San Juan dated thirty years prior, she brought the decrypted audio file directly into the ship's engine room to slice into a beat. The rhythmic hiss of the cooling valves fused instantly with a sobbing Solina string patch and an agonizingly slow highlife picking loop, capturing the precise second the crew realized no one on Earth was left answering their signals. During the track's climax, Santiago breaks her quiet spoken-word verse to scream across the sub-bass rumble, leaving the recording forever marked by the sound of hull-vibration rattle. "Que la carta llegó tarde pero el alma se nos fue" ("The letter arrived late but our souls have already departed").
